  "text": "None of the three solutions is convincingly correct, and I can't verify the true value from first principles here.\n\n**Solution 1 (answer 2)** asserts that the Noether–Lefschetz-type condition imposes \"two independent conditions\" without computing them. The argument is not checkable as written.\n\n**Solution 2 (answer 1)** has a more concrete mechanism: a $\\sigma$-anti-invariant class of square $-4$ cutting out a hypersurface. But the claim that this class forces the second family to be a hypersurface is asserted, not derived. Its nodal-locus claim also needs checking.\n\n**Solution 3 (answer 5)** has the most structure, and its setup is right: the fixed locus of $\\sigma$ is one rational curve, so $\\operatorname{rank} S_- = 11$ and the moduli space has dimension $20-11=9$. The problem is the dimension count for the order-3 case. For a non-symplectic eigenspace $E$ of dimension $n_6$ with signature $(2, n_6-2)$, the period domain has dimension $n_6-2$, not $n_6-1$. Using $n_6 = 5$, that gives a family of dimension 3 and codimension 6, not 5. The order-3 analysis also omits checks on the other eigenvalue multiplicities. So the answer 5 rests on an off-by-one step that appears to be wrong.\n\nSince none of the candidate answers (1, 2, 5) is supported by a verified argument, I can't select one with confidence.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
